.TH CCOL_SELECTABLE_FROM_CHAN 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_selectable_from_chan \- resolve a ccol_channel into a ccol_selectable for the calling thread and requested direction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_selectable ccol_selectable_from_chan(ccol_channel *ch, ccol_select_dir dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A ccol_channel is made of two circular queues. This function looks at
the ID of the calling thread and at the direction you ask for, and
picks which of the two queues to wrap:

  ccol_select_read; owner <- workers_to_owner_cq  (same as ccol_chan_recv_zc)
                    worker <- owner_to_workers_cq
  ccol_select_write; owner -> owner_to_workers_cq  (same as ccol_chan_send_zc)
                    worker -> workers_to_owner_cq

The queue is picked here, at the call site, so ccol_select() never
needs to look inside the opaque ccol_channel struct. The ccol_channel
pointer is not kept: the returned ccol_selectable holds only the
ccol_circular_queue pointer that was picked and the dir value, and it
watches that one queue whichever thread later waits on it or runs a
callback for it.

ccol_select() and ccol_event_loop only report that the queue is ready,
and the caller does the receive or the send itself.

When ccol_select() returns on the thread that built the selectable,
ccol_chan_try_recv_zc() and ccol_chan_try_send_zc() pick the same
queue, and so do ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() and ccol_circq_try_send_zc()
on sel.cq.

Inside a ccol_event_loop callback, use ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() and
ccol_circq_try_send_zc() on sel->cq and never a ccol_chan_* call. The
callback runs on a thread of the loop, which the channel treats as a
worker no matter which thread built the selectable, so when the owner
built it, a ccol_chan_* call in the callback reaches the other queue.
Take a selectable that the owner built for ccol_select_read, which
watches workers_to_owner_cq: a ccol_chan_try_recv_zc() inside
on_readable receives from owner_to_workers_cq instead. It takes a
message that the owner meant for its workers, and leaves the message
that triggered the callback in its queue.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch   Channel to resolve.  Must not be NULL.
dir  ccol_select_read or ccol_select_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A ccol_selectable of type ccol_selectable_circq that wraps the right
ccol_circular_queue for the calling thread and for the direction.

If ch is NULL, the result is a ccol_selectable_circq with a NULL
queue pointer, which ccol_select() refuses with ccol_invalid_args.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The direction is decided once, when this function is called, and not
again on each pass of ccol_select(). If the calling thread's role
changes between building the selectable and the actual ccol_select()
call, build the selectable again.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
The function reads ch->owner_tid and two pointer fields, which are
written once when the ccol_channel is created and never change after
that, so it is safe to call from any thread at the same time as any
other call.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* The owner watches the replies of its workers on a ccol_event_loop. */
static void on_reply(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg,
                     ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg) {
    (void)loop; (void)reg;
    c_message_t msg;
    /* sel->cq, and not ccol_chan_try_recv_zc(): this runs on a thread of
       the loop, which the channel counts as a worker. */
    while (ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(sel->cq, &msg) == ccol_success)
        handle_reply(arg, &msg);
}

ccol_event_handlers_t h = { .on_readable = on_reply };
ccol_event_reg reg = ccol_event_loop_add(
    loop, ccol_selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read), h, ctx, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_select (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_chan_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_chan_send_zc (3)
